post_install() {
  udevadm control --reload 2>/dev/null
  udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=misc --action=change 2>/dev/null
  cat <<'EOF'
==> Beamer can be driven by another machine as it is: its rule for /dev/uinput is in force,
    which lets every program you run type and click as you.

==> To DRIVE another machine from Hyprland, sway or another wlroots compositor, Beamer must
    read the keyboards and mice in /dev/input. That rule is NOT in force. It lets every program
    you run, not Beamer alone, read every key typed on this machine, passwords included, and
    take the keyboard away from the desktop. Turn it on only on a machine that drives others:

      sudo install -m 644 /usr/share/doc/beamer/70-beamer-input.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
      sudo udevadm control --reload
      sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=input --action=change

    To take it back: remove /etc/udev/rules.d/70-beamer-input.rules and run the last two again.
    GNOME and KDE Plasma need neither rule.

==> A firewall must let in TCP 24820 and UDP 24821, or pairing fails:
      sudo ufw allow 24820/tcp && sudo ufw allow 24821/udp
EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
  udevadm control --reload 2>/dev/null
  true
}
